// Practice quiz: Spring Data JPA with Hibernate
// Minimum release, November 2026: author-checked, no independent review. Formative, low-stakes retention practice. Titles name the objective id each item practises.

$CATEGORY: spring-boot/spring-data-jpa/spring-data-jpa-with-hibernate

::o1 provider::Which project actually issues SQL in the Spring Data JPA stack? {
=Hibernate#Correct. It is the JPA provider.
~Spring Boot#Not quite. Boot configures the stack.
~The Jakarta Persistence specification#Not quite. A specification does not run.
~Maven#Not quite. Maven builds the project.
}

::o1 repository implementation::You declare a repository interface with no class. Where does the implementation come from? {
=Spring Data generates it at runtime#Correct. You write only the interface.
~You must write it later#Not quite. That is the point of Spring Data\: you do not.
~The database#Not quite. Databases do not implement Java interfaces.
~The Java compiler#Not quite. The compiler does not generate repositories.
}

::o2 embedded default::H2 is on the classpath, and no datasource URL is set. What does Boot configure? {
=An embedded in-memory database#Correct. It uses a generated unique name.
~A PostgreSQL connection#Not quite. It has no URL to connect to.
~Nothing, and startup fails#Not quite. Embedded support handles this case.
~A file database in your home folder#Not quite. The default is in memory.
}

::o2 ddl default::With an embedded database and no schema manager, what is the default ddl-auto? {
=create-drop#Correct. The schema is built at startup and dropped at shutdown.
~none#Not quite. That applies for non-embedded databases.
~validate#Not quite. It is not the default.
~update#Not quite. It is not the default.
}

::o3 JDBC compared::What does a repository call remove compared with hand-written JDBC? {
=The connection, statement and result mapping code#Correct. That boilerplate moves into the framework.
~SQL itself#Not quite. Hibernate still issues SQL.
~The database#Not quite. The database is still there.
~Transactions#Not quite. They still exist, even if managed for you.
}

